\title{The session cap of the machine is 1 MiB}
\taxon{Decision}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{machine}
\tag{performance}
\meta{status}{accepted}
\meta{created}{2026-11-24}
\meta{origin}{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} accepted and in force. Re-measured with the default switch set of the wave-6 integration; the cap stays.}

\p{\strong{Context.} A [warm session](design-warm-sessions) keeps what the machine derived across accepted declarations and is forgotten when its footprint passes a cap. The design set the cap at 16 MiB and asked for a sweep over 8, 16, 32 and 64 MiB by CPU time once the machine ran Std. The first integration of the machine found that range wrong by an order of magnitude: with the switches \code{\verb~|warm,gated,expect~}, one worker and the native image, the median CPU time of three rounds was}
\table{
\tr{\th{Driver's cap}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{Cedar slice}}
\tr{\td{2 MiB}\td{7.71 s}\td{14.08 s}\td{3.32 s}}
\tr{\td{4 MiB}\td{7.78 s}\td{13.80 s}\td{3.40 s}}
\tr{\td{8 MiB}\td{8.23 s}\td{14.59 s}\td{3.58 s}}
\tr{\td{16 MiB, the design's default}\td{9.23 s}\td{16.32 s}\td{4.15 s}}
\tr{\td{no warm session}\td{8.13 s}\td{13.72 s}\td{3.40 s}}
}
\p{With 16 MiB a warm session was slower than none, while the instruction count went the other way (Init 118.0 G at 2 MiB, 97.9 G at 16 MiB). The driver's cap for the machine became 4 MiB. The owner of the machine's facade then gave the machine a cap of its own, because the best value is a property of the machine's tables, which must stay in the processor's caches, and not of the driver's memory account.}

\p{\strong{Decision.} The machine forgets a warm session before a declaration once its engine bytes exceed \strong{1 MiB} (\code{\verb~|-X machine.session-kib=1024~}; 0 means none). The driver's cap for the machine stays 4 MiB (\code{\verb~|-X session-mib~}); the two compose and the smaller resets first.}

\p{\strong{Evidence.} The sweep was repeated with the default switch set of the wave-6 integration: native image, 8 workers, pipelined load, each row inside a quiet-core window, wall clock and process CPU time of the median later pass of four (Mathlib: of two).}
\table{
\tr{\th{Cap}\th{Init}\th{Std}\th{Cedar slice}\th{reachability}\th{cedar}\th{Mathlib}}
\tr{\td{512 KiB}\td{0.82 / 5.22}\td{1.53 / 9.92}\td{0.43 / 2.36}\td{0.66 / 1.58}\td{2.10 / 13.73}\td{29.20 / 202.6}}
\tr{\td{\strong{1 MiB}}\td{0.81 / 5.26}\td{1.51 / 10.09}\td{0.46 / 2.40}\td{0.62 / 1.64}\td{2.09 / 13.99}\td{29.28 / 204.8}}
\tr{\td{2 MiB}\td{0.82 / 5.49}\td{1.53 / 10.48}\td{0.47 / 2.45}\td{0.61 / 1.61}\td{2.15 / 14.51}\td{30.55 / 211.8}}
\tr{\td{4 MiB}\td{0.85 / 5.58}\td{1.62 / 10.84}\td{0.52 / 2.61}\td{0.67 / 1.62}\td{2.46 / 14.92}\td{30.26 / 212.2}}
}
\ul{
\li{512 KiB and 1 MiB are within 2 \% of each other on every corpus. There is one row each, so they cannot be ranked.}
\li{2 MiB costs 3 to 4 \% of CPU time on Init, Std, cedar and Mathlib; 4 MiB costs 4 to 7 \%, and 17 \% of the wall clock on cedar.}
\li{A profile (P8) had found 17 \% more ticks on a Mathlib slice at 1 MiB than at 4 MiB and asked whether the cap was too small. The answer is the wall clock of all of Mathlib: 29.3 s per warm pass at 1 MiB against 30.6 and 30.3 s at 2 and 4 MiB.}
\li{A later profile (P9) confirms it on con-leche with one worker on quiet cores: 74.2 M ticks at 1 MiB, 67.4 M at 4 MiB, 62.0 M at 16 MiB and 58.6 M at 64 MiB, against 7.86, 8.02, 8.61 and 10.16 s of thread CPU time in the checks. The ticks fall and the time rises: 106 ns per tick at 1 MiB, 174 at 64 MiB.}
}

\p{\strong{Consequences.}}
\ul{
\li{The choice is against the instruction count, which is the arena's metric and not this project's: a larger cap finds more memo hits and executes fewer instructions, and is slower.}
\li{512 KiB is not adopted. It shows 1 to 2 \% less CPU time in one row each at equal wall clock; adopting it needs three rounds.}
\li{Families of sibling declarations with shared subterms are derived again after a reset. On con-leche one such proof costs the machine about 44,800 ticks where gruszka, a leading checker of the arena, answers it in about 430 from what the previous sibling left in its session. As the sessions are built today these units are not a prize in time.}
\li{The base sizes of the session tables were reduced on the same reasoning, see [warm sessions](design-warm-sessions).}
}

\p{Related: [staging switches](design-switches), [the default switch set](dec-LZFX), [the metric](dec-7QMU). Detail: [design-switches-document](design-switches-document) section "Sessions", [rep-machine-M6](rep-machine-M6) (F24), [rep-machine-INT](rep-machine-INT), \src{rlvm-driver/README.md} section "Session cap", [rep-P9](rep-P9) section 4.5. Source: [the speed wave](ses-CVTE).}

\p{Findings, examples and open problems: [the best cap is set by the processor cache](ins-FJ5R), [sibling proofs and what a longer session answers](ex-UT0I).}
